from engine import Author
A=Author('28');add=A.add
add('6.2.1-01','Radioactive-source work minimises exposure time, increases appropriate distance and uses suitable shielding and containment.','Sources require controlled, labelled storage and authorised handling.','In a supervised school demonstration, the teacher uses the approved source holder and handling tools, keeps sources away from the body and returns them to secure designated storage. Do not touch, open or ingest source material. Shielding choice depends on radiation type and the source arrangement, so an unspecified thick object is not a complete safety plan. Learners can study supplied readings and simulations without handling sources.','Describe the basic exposure-reduction principles for a supervised radioactive-source demonstration.','Minimise time near the source, maximise suitable distance and use appropriate shielding and controlled storage.')
add('6.2.2-01','Background radiation is detected even without a nearby deliberate laboratory source.','Contributors include cosmic radiation, radioactive rocks and soil, radon and naturally occurring isotopes in living matter.','A counter reading with the test source removed estimates background for that setup. Random variation means one short reading is not an exact fixed value. Measure over a suitable interval and convert to a rate before subtracting it from a source-plus-background rate. Compare rates in matching units.','State two causes of background radiation.','Cosmic radiation and radioactive materials in rocks or soil.')
add('6.2.2-02','Radiation detectors reveal interactions rather than showing radioactive nuclei directly.','Cloud chambers, G-M counters and photographic film provide different observable responses.','A cloud chamber makes tracks through condensation on ions, a counter records pulses associated with ionisation, and film records chemical changes after development. No one displayed count automatically identifies alpha, beta or gamma; detector design, absorption tests and controlled geometry help distinguish them.','Explain the common detection link between a cloud chamber and a G-M counter.','Link both to ionisation caused by radiation interactions.~The chamber turns it into visible droplet tracks, while the counter turns it into electrical pulses.')
add('6.2.2-02-M01','A cloud chamber reveals charged-particle tracks through condensation around ions.','Track appearance can support interpretation but is not a direct photograph of the particle itself.','An alpha track is commonly short, thick and relatively straight in the intended comparison, while an electron track can be thinner and irregular. Gamma photons can produce secondary charged particles whose tracks are observed. Use teacher-prepared observations rather than constructing a cold or hazardous apparatus without appropriate supervision.','Describe how a cloud chamber makes radiation effects visible.','Vapour condenses around ions produced along charged-particle paths, forming visible droplet tracks.')
add('6.2.2-02-M02','A G-M tube produces electrical pulses when radiation interactions ionise its gas and trigger a discharge.','A scaler counts pulses or an instrument reports a count rate.','A thin window helps some charged particles enter, while gamma detection efficiency can be lower and depends on geometry and construction. Background must be measured separately. A count rate is an instrument response, not necessarily equal to total source activity because only some emissions reach and trigger the detector.','Describe how a G-M counter detects radiation.','Radiation interactions produce ionisation in the tube, triggering pulses that are counted.')
add('6.2.2-02-M03','Photographic film can record radiation exposure through a chemical change.','After controlled development, darkening indicates interaction with the sensitive material.','A film badge can accumulate exposure information over time, while filters can help interpret the radiation reaching it. Unlike an electronic counter, it does not ordinarily give a live pulse display during exposure. Appropriate calibration is needed before converting a response into an exposure estimate.','Describe how photographic film detects radiation.','Radiation changes the film’s sensitive material, producing darkening after development.')
add('6.2.3-01','Radioactive decay is random for individual unstable nuclei.','A large collection has statistically predictable average decay behaviour.','The exact decay time of one nucleus cannot be predicted from its age alone. Equal counting intervals can give different counts even when the expected rate is almost unchanged. Random emission direction also means a small detector intercepts only part of the total output. These fluctuations do not invalidate the repeatable half-life model for a large sample.','State what random decay means for an individual nucleus.','Its exact decay time cannot be predicted.')
add('6.2.3-02','Alpha, beta-minus and gamma emissions differ in nature, charge and interaction.','The school comparison is alpha strongly ionising with low penetration, beta intermediate and gamma more penetrating with lower direct ionisation density.','An alpha is a helium nucleus with charge +2e and relative mass about 4. A beta-minus particle is an electron with charge −e and a mass much smaller than a nucleon. Gamma is a neutral electromagnetic photon with no rest mass. The comparison is qualitative and depends on energy and material; sufficient shielding attenuates rather than giving a magic absolute stopping thickness for every source.','Explain the link between alpha and gamma’s typical ionising and penetrating effects.','Alpha deposits energy densely and is strongly ionising over a short range.~Gamma interacts less frequently along a path, so it is typically more penetrating but less densely ionising.')
add('6.2.3-02-M01','An alpha particle contains two protons and two neutrons and has charge +2e.','Its strong interactions give high ionisation density and a short range in ordinary materials.','Paper or the outer surface layer of skin can stop typical alpha particles in the school comparison, but an alpha emitter inside the body can be hazardous because it deposits energy nearby. Low external penetration therefore must not be called harmlessness.','Explain why alpha radiation is strongly ionising but has low penetration.','Its charge and interactions transfer energy densely to nearby material.~It loses its energy over a short path and is stopped relatively readily.')
add('6.2.3-02-M02','Beta-minus radiation consists of fast electrons with charge −e.','It generally has intermediate penetration and ionisation in the named school comparison.','Thin metal or suitable plastic can substantially attenuate beta radiation depending on energy. Electrons have much smaller mass than alpha particles and can show strong deflection in fields. The beta electron originates in nuclear decay rather than being a pre-existing electron stored inside a neutron as a tiny planetary object.','Explain why beta radiation differs from alpha in field response and penetration.','Beta-minus particles are much lighter negative electrons rather than positive helium nuclei.~They are deflected in the opposite direction and generally penetrate farther in the stated comparison.')
add('6.2.3-02-M03','Gamma radiation consists of electromagnetic photons with no net electric charge.','It is typically highly penetrating and requires substantial attenuation shielding compared with alpha and beta.','Gamma can ionise indirectly when interactions create energetic charged particles. Lead or concrete can reduce the transmitted intensity, with effectiveness depending on thickness and photon energy. Neutrality means an ordinary electric or magnetic field does not bend its path in the school model; it does not mean gamma cannot transfer energy.','Explain why gamma is not deflected by the electric-field setup used to separate alpha and beta.','Gamma photons have no electric charge.~They therefore do not experience the charge-dependent electric force that deflects alpha and beta.')
add('6.2.3-03','Electric and magnetic fields distinguish charged radiation from neutral gamma in the basic deflection model.','Alpha and beta-minus deflect oppositely when launched in the same direction through the same field arrangement.','For an electric field, force follows the positive-charge direction for alpha and the opposite direction for beta-minus. For a magnetic field, charge sign and velocity direction both matter. Gamma continues straight in the intended field-only model. Relative curvature also depends on speed and momentum, so charge alone does not set an exact radius.','Explain how field deflection can distinguish gamma from alpha and beta-minus.','Link alpha and beta-minus charges to field-dependent deflection.~Gamma is neutral and remains undeflected by the stated electric or magnetic field.')
add('6.2.3-03-M01','In a uniform electric field, alpha force is along the field and beta-minus force is opposite it.','Gamma has no electric deflection in this model.','If a beam travels rightward between an upper positive plate and lower negative plate, the field points downward. Alpha curves downward toward the negative plate, beta-minus upward toward the positive plate, and gamma continues straight. Draw the plate signs before choosing curvature.','Explain the paths of alpha, beta-minus and gamma between an upper positive plate and lower negative plate for an initially rightward beam.','The downward field deflects positive alpha downward and negative beta-minus upward.~Neutral gamma continues straight in the field model.')
add('6.2.3-03-M02','Magnetic force on charged moving radiation is perpendicular to its motion and the field.','Opposite charge signs produce opposite deflections for the same initial velocity and field.','For a rightward beam and field into the page, positive alpha curves upward. Beta-minus curves downward because its charge is negative, and gamma is undeflected. Use conventional-current direction or reverse the positive-charge result for electrons. The curved trajectory does not mean the magnetic field is doing work through a force along the velocity in this ideal account.','Explain the initial deflection of a rightward alpha and beta-minus beam in a field directed into the page.','Positive alpha is forced upward by the field-motion relation.~Negative beta-minus is forced downward; gamma has no such magnetic deflection.')
add('6.2.3-04','Ionisation removes or adds electrons to produce charged atoms or molecules.','Different radiation types deposit energy with different spatial densities in the school comparison.','An alpha particle can create many ions along a short path, explaining its thick cloud-chamber trace and strong local biological effect when internal. Gamma can travel farther between interactions but still produce ionisation through secondary particles. Distinguish ionising effect from penetration rather than assuming the most penetrating radiation must create the densest short track.','Explain why greater penetration does not mean greater ionisation density along a short path.','Radiation that interacts less frequently can travel farther.~Those fewer interactions can produce a lower local ionisation density than a strongly interacting short-range particle.')
add('6.2.4-01','Radioactive decay is spontaneous change of an unstable nucleus with emission.','Nuclear-number balances identify the resulting nuclide change.','Alpha reduces A by four and Z by two; beta-minus keeps A and raises Z by one; gamma emission leaves both unchanged while reducing nuclear excitation energy. As examples, ²³⁸₉₂U → ²³⁴₉₀Th + ⁴₂He and ¹⁴₆C → ¹⁴₇N + ⁰₋₁e show the school number balances. The beta-minus antineutrino is omitted from that simplified display but contributes neither A nor charge.','Explain how a decay equation shows whether an emission changes the element.','Link element identity to Z in the balanced equation.~Alpha and beta-minus change Z, while gamma leaves Z and A unchanged.')
add('6.2.4-01-M01','Radioactive decay is spontaneous transformation of an unstable nucleus with emission of particles or radiation.','It is a nuclear process rather than ordinary electron-shell chemistry.','A nucleus can move toward a more stable configuration without an external trigger for that individual event. The event is random, while the population follows statistical patterns. Ordinary school changes of temperature or pressure do not provide a practical on-off switch for the decay.','Define radioactive decay.','It is spontaneous change of an unstable nucleus with emission of particles or radiation.')
add('6.2.4-01-M02','Nucleon-number and charge conservation determine daughter nuclear numbers.','Alpha removes (A,Z) = (4,2), beta-minus adds one to daughter Z at unchanged A, and gamma changes neither.','Starting with A = 222 and Z = 86, alpha leaves A = 218 and Z = 84. Check the emitted particle on the same side of the equation as the daughter so both sums match the parent. Do not subtract four from Z or add two to A by confusing their notation positions.','Calculate daughter A and Z after alpha emission from a nucleus with A = 222 and Z = 86.','A = 222 − 4 = 218.~Z = 86 − 2 = 84.')
add('6.2.5-01','Half-life is the time for half the unstable nuclei in a large sample to decay on average.','The same interval halves the expected activity of a single-isotope sample under the simple model.','The next half-life removes half of the remaining nuclei rather than the same absolute number as before. After two half-lives one quarter remains, and after three one eighth. An individual nucleus does not have a scheduled appointment to decay exactly one half-life after it was formed.','Define half-life.','It is the time taken for half the radioactive nuclei in a large sample to decay on average.')
add('6.2.5-02','Repeated half-lives give a geometric decrease of remaining nuclei or source activity.','Measured count rate must be corrected for background before using a simple source-halving model.','Original curve data give background-corrected source rates of 80, 40, 20 and 10 counts/s at 0, 6, 12 and 18 min. The half-life is 6 min. If a constant background of 5 counts/s were added, the measured sequence would be 85, 45, 25 and 15 counts/s; halving those totals would be wrong. Read several halving intervals on a smooth curve and compare them.','Calculate the half-life using a decay curve with background-corrected rates 80, 40, 20 and 10 counts/s at 0, 6, 12 and 18 min.','The source rate falls by a factor of two over each 6 min interval.~The half-life is 6 min.')
x=[i*.3 for i in range(61)];A.plot('6.2.5-02',x,{'Source count rate':[80*2**(-t/6) for t in x]},'Time / min','Background-corrected count rate / s⁻¹','Original idealised decay data')
